Chapter: Cat

 The cat's PLACEMENT, and only that.

 A partial port of wasm/cat.zig: the `Cat` record, `CAT_ALONG` and `make`. That is
 what world.zig needs to author a segment, and it is arithmetic over five
 constants with no dependencies at all.

 MOST OF cat.zig IS DEFERRED AND THE REASON IS NOT DIFFICULTY. `inDanger` is
 arithmetic over three constants and is ported below, because the rider's throttle
 gate reads it every frame. Its `state`,
 `state` and `draw` reach `cat_frames.zig`, which is 434 KB of BAKED polygons --
 and baked means GENERATED, by ops/bake_cat running the real TypeScript drawing
 code through a recorder. NOTES section 5 scopes that out and says how it should
 arrive when it does: teach the baker a Codex emitter and the data ports itself.
 Hand-porting 434 KB of generated coordinates would be the wrong shape of work
 and would rot the moment the baker ran again.

 So this chapter is honest about being a slice: the cat can be PLACED but not yet
 animated or drawn.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

  cat-height : Real
  cat-height = 1.7

 The desired spot down the road. world.zig rounds it up to just past a tree, so a
 tree stands between the rider and the cat.

  cat-along : Real
  cat-along = 105.0

 Clearance beyond the roadside tree line, and how far past the rounding tree it
 sits.

  cat-road-gap : Real
  cat-road-gap = 1.5

  cat-beyond-tree : Real
  cat-beyond-tree = 2.0

 Head-centre x in the unit frame, from the cat's anatomy.

  cat-head-x : Real
  cat-head-x = 0.0 - 0.48

 The hind paw's reach in the landing pose, and how far past the left edge it
 settles.

  land-hind-reach : Real
  land-hind-reach = 0.70 * cat-height

  grass-toehold : Real
  grass-toehold = 0.3

Section: Placement

  Cat = record {
    along : Real,
    start-across : Real,
    mid-across : Real,
    end-across : Real,
    height : Real
  }

 The waiting spot is right of the road; mid-across is the anchor offset that puts
 the head centre on the lane; end-across puts the hind paw barely onto the left
 grass.

  cat-make : Real, Real, Real -> Cat
  cat-make (lane-half) (tree-offset) (tree-along) =
    let tree-x = lane-half + tree-offset
    in Cat {
      along = tree-along + cat-beyond-tree,
      start-across = tree-x + cat-road-gap,
      mid-across = (0.0 - cat-head-x) * cat-height,
      end-across = (0.0 - (lane-half + grass-toehold)) - land-hind-reach,
      height = cat-height
    }

Section: The Crossing Window

 The cat's crossing takes this many frames end to end: ten to enter the road,
 twenty-four frozen in the headlight, and four to escape -- coil, airborne twice,
 land, collapse. The rider's throttle gate reads it, which is why these three are
 here while the poses they name are not.

  enters-road-steps : Real
  enters-road-steps = 10.0

  frozen-steps : Real
  frozen-steps = 24.0

  escapes-steps : Real
  escapes-steps = 4.0

  cross-frames : Real
  cross-frames = enters-road-steps + frozen-steps + escapes-steps

 Metres of clear road kept between rider and cat.

  road-buffer : Real
  road-buffer = 3.0

 Is the cat still ahead, beyond the buffer, AND inside the crossing window -- that
 is, crossing in front of us right now? The rider's accel gate holds the throttle
 while this holds: he stops CLOSING on the cat but never brakes.

  cat-in-danger : Real, Real -> Boolean
  cat-in-danger (gap-along) (v) =
    let e = gap-along - road-buffer
    in if e > 0.0 then e <= cross-frames * v else False

Chapter: DeviceMath

 We say:

Section: Clamps and Selection

  real-min : Real, Real -> Real
  real-min (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

  real-max : Real, Real -> Real
  real-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  real-abs : Real -> Real
  real-abs (x) = if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - x else x

Section: Square Root

  real-sqrt : Real -> Real
  real-sqrt (x) =
    if x <= 0.0 then 0.0
    else dm-sqrt-scaled x 1.0 700

  dm-sqrt-scaled : Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  dm-sqrt-scaled (x) (s) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then s * dm-sqrt-core x
    else if x >= 4.0 then dm-sqrt-scaled (x / 4.0) (s * 2.0) (fuel - 1)
    else if x < 0.25 then dm-sqrt-scaled (x * 4.0) (s * 0.5) (fuel - 1)
    else s * dm-sqrt-core x

  dm-sqrt-core : Real -> Real
  dm-sqrt-core (r) =
    let g0 = (r + 1.0) * 0.5
    in let g1 = (g0 + r / g0) * 0.5
    in let g2 = (g1 + r / g1) * 0.5
    in let g3 = (g2 + r / g2) * 0.5
    in let g4 = (g3 + r / g3) * 0.5
    in (g4 + r / g4) * 0.5

Section: Sine and Cosine

  dm-two-pi : Real
  dm-two-pi = 6.283185307179586

  dm-pi : Real
  dm-pi = 3.141592653589793

  dm-half-pi : Real
  dm-half-pi = 1.570796326794897

  dm-reduce : Real -> Real
  dm-reduce (x) =
    let k = real-from-int (real-to-int (x / dm-two-pi))
    in let r = x - k * dm-two-pi
    in if r > dm-pi then r - dm-two-pi
       else if r < 0.0 - dm-pi then r + dm-two-pi
       else r

  dm-fold-quadrant : Real -> Real
  dm-fold-quadrant (r) =
    if r > dm-half-pi then dm-pi - r
    else if r < 0.0 - dm-half-pi then 0.0 - dm-pi - r
    else r

  dm-sin-poly : Real -> Real
  dm-sin-poly (r) =
    let r2 = r * r
    in let r3 = r2 * r
    in let r5 = r3 * r2
    in let r7 = r5 * r2
    in let r9 = r7 * r2
    in let r11 = r9 * r2
    in r - r3 / 6.0 + r5 / 120.0 - r7 / 5040.0 + r9 / 362880.0 - r11 / 39916800.0

  real-sin : Real -> Real
  real-sin (x) = dm-sin-poly (dm-fold-quadrant (dm-reduce x))

  real-cos : Real -> Real
  real-cos (x) = dm-sin-poly (dm-fold-quadrant (dm-reduce (x + dm-half-pi)))

Chapter: Num

 The Real primitives Codex's foreword does not have.

 `Gpu chapter DeviceMath` is the only Real chapter in the foreword and its whole
 surface is min, max, abs, sqrt, sin and cos. There is no rounding, no floor and
 no modulo, and the foreword's other numeric chapters -- Cordic, Easing, Color,
 GameCamera, Numeric -- are all INTEGER milli-units, so they answer a different
 question. Everything here is a gap upstream rather than a hole in the zig plug,
 and unlike `Safari chapter Trig` none of it is a stand-in for something that
 already exists.

 EVERY FUNCTION HERE NEEDS real-to-int, so none of it could have been written
 before 2026-08-29 -- see PORTING_NOTES A1 and plugs-backlog row 2.03. That is
 the honest reason this chapter did not exist earlier: the port had no way to
 reach the integers at all.

 We say:

Section: Rounding

 Zig's @round goes to nearest and ties AWAY FROM ZERO, which truncation does not
 give, so the half is added before the conversion and the sign is handled. The
 colour channels this rounds are all in 0..255 and never take the negative arm --
 it is written because a round that is wrong for half its domain is a trap for
 the next caller.

  round-real : Real -> Real
  round-real (x) =
    if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - real-from-int (real-to-int (0.5 - x))
    else real-from-int (real-to-int (x + 0.5))

 real-to-int truncates toward zero, so for a negative non-integral value it lands
 ABOVE its argument and floor is one less. An exact integer is its own floor in
 both arms, which is why the test is `>` and not `>=`.

  floor-real : Real -> Real
  floor-real (x) =
    let t = real-from-int (real-to-int x)
    in if t > x then t - 1.0 else t

Section: Modulo

 Zig's @mod is the FLOORED modulo -- its result takes the sign of the divisor --
 and that is not the truncated remainder. Floored is x - m * floor (x / m), and
 this is the faithful reading of what tower.zig asks for.

 BUT THE ONE CALLER CANNOT TELL THE DIFFERENCE, and that is worth stating rather
 than implying coverage this does not have. tower.zig writes

     @mod(@mod(phase, P) + P, P)

 which looks like belt and braces over an operator that is already non-negative,
 and is in fact exactly what makes the two semantics AGREE. Floored gives m in
 [0, P) directly and the second pass is a no-op. Truncated gives r in (-P, P);
 adding P lands in (0, 2P); the second pass takes P off again when it overflowed
 and leaves it alone when it did not -- and r + P is precisely floored's answer
 whenever r was negative. So the double form is semantics-agnostic by
 construction.

 MEASURED, not reasoned about after the fact: replacing this with the truncated
 remainder leaves TowerCheck entirely green, including its negative-phase case.
 An earlier draft of this note claimed tower.zig "relies on" the floored form and
 that the two "disagree there". Both were wrong, and the red test said so.

 Floored stays because it is what @mod means and the next caller may not wrap its
 argument twice. But nothing in this port grades the choice today.

  mod-real : Real, Real -> Real
  mod-real (x) (m) = x - m * floor-real (x / m)

Section: The Exponential

 rider.zig's shoulder brake decays as exp(-n / 20) over up to 2000 frames, so the
 argument reaches -100 and a bare Taylor series is useless there. The standard
 reduction: take k as the nearest integer to x / ln 2, so the remainder r is
 within half a ln 2 of zero, sum the series there, and scale by 2^k.

 THIS NEEDS real-to-int TWICE OVER -- once to round k, once to count the doublings
 -- so like everything else here it could not have been written before the
 conversion emitters landed.

  ln2 : Real
  ln2 = 0.6931471805599453

 Twelve terms over |r| <= 0.347 leaves a remainder around 1e-17, which is under an
 f64 ulp at 1, so the scaling cannot magnify anything that matters.

  exp-poly : Real -> Real
  exp-poly (r) =
    let t1 = r
    in let t2 = t1 * r / 2.0
    in let t3 = t2 * r / 3.0
    in let t4 = t3 * r / 4.0
    in let t5 = t4 * r / 5.0
    in let t6 = t5 * r / 6.0
    in let t7 = t6 * r / 7.0
    in let t8 = t7 * r / 8.0
    in let t9 = t8 * r / 9.0
    in let t10 = t9 * r / 10.0
    in let t11 = t10 * r / 11.0
    in let t12 = t11 * r / 12.0
    in 1.0 + t1 + t2 + t3 + t4 + t5 + t6 + t7 + t8 + t9 + t10 + t11 + t12

 2^k by repeated multiplication rather than by assembling an exponent field: the
 bit-level route would need real-to-bits, which this arm still has no emitter for,
 and k is small enough here that the loop is exact anyway -- every step multiplies
 by a power of two, which is exact in binary until it underflows.

  pow2-up : Integer, Real -> Real
  pow2-up (k) (acc) = if k <= 0 then acc else pow2-up (k - 1) (acc * 2.0)

  pow2-down : Integer, Real -> Real
  pow2-down (k) (acc) = if k >= 0 then acc else pow2-down (k + 1) (acc * 0.5)

  pow2-int : Integer -> Real
  pow2-int (k) = if k >= 0 then pow2-up k 1.0 else pow2-down k 1.0

  exp-real : Real -> Real
  exp-real (x) =
    let k = real-to-int (round-real (x / ln2))
    in exp-poly (x - real-from-int k * ln2) * pow2-int k

Chapter: Trig
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath

 Sine, cosine, tangent and arc tangent in Real.

 THIS CHAPTER IS A STAND-IN. Codex already has these in `Gpu chapter DeviceMath`
 and this is the same algorithm -- an argument reduction, a quadrant fold, and an
 11th-order Taylor polynomial -- reproduced only because DeviceMath cannot be
 transpiled to zig at all: its dm-reduce calls real-from-int, and the zig plug has
 no emitter for it. The day that emitter lands, every function here is deleted and
 the citers cite DeviceMath instead. Nothing else in the port should grow a
 dependency on these names.

 The one deliberate difference from DeviceMath: dm-reduce divides by two-pi and
 converts back through the integers, which is exactly the call this arm cannot
 emit, so `wrap` subtracts in a bounded loop instead. That is slower and it is
 exact for the arguments this game has -- yaws, headings and tier angles, all
 within a few turns of zero -- but it is NOT a general reduction, and it must not
 outlive the emitter it works around.

 We say:

Section: Constants

  pi : Real
  pi = 3.141592653589793

  two-pi : Real
  two-pi = 6.283185307179586

  half-pi : Real
  half-pi = 1.570796326794897

  deg : Real
  deg = 0.017453292519943295

Section: Sine and Cosine

  sin-poly : Real -> Real
  sin-poly (r) =
    let r2 = r * r
    in let r3 = r2 * r
    in let r5 = r3 * r2
    in let r7 = r5 * r2
    in let r9 = r7 * r2
    in let r11 = r9 * r2
    in r - r3 / 6.0 + r5 / 120.0 - r7 / 5040.0 + r9 / 362880.0 - r11 / 39916800.0

  fold-quadrant : Real -> Real
  fold-quadrant (r) =
    if r > half-pi then pi - r
    else if r < 0.0 - half-pi then 0.0 - pi - r
    else r

  wrap : Real, Integer -> Real
  wrap (x) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then x
    else if x > pi then wrap (x - two-pi) (fuel - 1)
    else if x < 0.0 - pi then wrap (x + two-pi) (fuel - 1)
    else x

  r-sin : Real -> Real
  r-sin (x) = sin-poly (fold-quadrant (wrap x 64))

  r-cos : Real -> Real
  r-cos (x) = r-sin (x + half-pi)

  r-tan : Real -> Real
  r-tan (x) = r-sin x / r-cos x

Section: Arc Tangent

 THIS ONE IS NOT A STAND-IN. `Gpu chapter DeviceMath` has no atan at all -- its
 whole surface is min, max, abs, sqrt, sin and cos -- and the foreword's other
 arc tangents (Math chapter Cordic, Math chapter Geodesic) are INTEGER milli-unit
 routines, one of which is recorded in PORTING_NOTES A4 as four times less
 accurate than it claims. So a Real atan is a gap in Codex's foreword rather than
 a hole in the zig plug, and it does not go away when the rest of this chapter is
 retired in favour of DeviceMath.

 The method is argument HALVING, not a Taylor series applied directly: the series
 for atan converges hopelessly slowly near |t| = 1, which is exactly where the
 mountains sample it. The half-angle identity

     atan t = 2 * atan (t / (1 + sqrt (1 + t*t)))

 shrinks the argument geometrically, so four applications take |t| <= 1 down to
 about 0.06, where a six-term series is good to roughly 1e-14 and the sixteen it
 is multiplied back by cannot undo that. Above |t| = 1 the reciprocal identity
 folds it back into range first; the screen's outer columns at a pulled-in focal
 do reach |t| near 3, so that branch is live and not defensive.

  atan-half-step : Real -> Real
  atan-half-step (t) = t / (1.0 + real-sqrt (1.0 + t * t))

  atan-halve : Real, Integer -> Real
  atan-halve (t) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then t
    else atan-halve (atan-half-step t) (n - 1)

  atan-series : Real -> Real
  atan-series (u) =
    let u2 = u * u
    in let u3 = u2 * u
    in let u5 = u3 * u2
    in let u7 = u5 * u2
    in let u9 = u7 * u2
    in let u11 = u9 * u2
    in u - u3 / 3.0 + u5 / 5.0 - u7 / 7.0 + u9 / 9.0 - u11 / 11.0

 Four halvings, so the series result is scaled back by 2^4.

  atan-core : Real -> Real
  atan-core (t) = 16.0 * atan-series (atan-halve t 4)

  r-atan : Real -> Real
  r-atan (t) =
    if t > 1.0 then half-pi - atan-core (1.0 / t)
    else if t < 0.0 - 1.0 then (0.0 - half-pi) - atan-core (1.0 / t)
    else atan-core t

 The TWO-argument arc tangent, which is not a convenience wrapper: it is the one
 that knows which quadrant the point is in, and gaze.zig needs that because the
 pig it is aiming at can be behind the rider as well as ahead. Dividing first and
 calling r-atan would fold two quadrants onto one.

 The x = 0 arms are exact rather than a limit, and the y = 0, x = 0 case answers
 zero, which is what std.math.atan2 does with two positive zeros.

  r-atan2 : Real, Real -> Real
  r-atan2 (y) (x) =
    if x > 0.0 then r-atan (y / x)
    else if x < 0.0 then (if y >= 0.0 then r-atan (y / x) + pi else r-atan (y / x) - pi)
    else if y > 0.0 then half-pi
    else if y < 0.0 then 0.0 - half-pi
    else 0.0

 Zig's std.math.sign, which answers zero AT zero rather than picking a side.

  r-sign : Real -> Real
  r-sign (x) = if x > 0.0 then 1.0 else (if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - 1.0 else 0.0)

Chapter: World
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Cat
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The road network as data: a chain of straight segments joined by turns. Each
 segment carries its length, width, tree rows, cow herd, pigs, and its EXIT turn --
 angle, direction, and the segment it leads to -- and build-world accumulates each
 segment's heading relative to north.

 A port of wasm/world.zig.

 NO RIDER, NO PROJECTION, NO DRAWING. This is the first chapter in the port that
 draws nothing at all: it is authored data plus the deterministic generators that
 expand it. That makes it seam 2 in NOTES' terms -- the seam that compares
 EXACTLY, because there is no arithmetic here whose width could differ except the
 jitter sines and the turn angles.

 The route is the full nineteen segments of the TypeScript. The baseline is
 deliberately boring -- all-green trees and a cow herd -- so the rare departures
 surprise, and novelty is front-loaded: the cat debuts on segment 2, pigs on 3,
 gold trees on 4. Segment 19 terminates; the rider arrives and stops.

 PIGS AND SAFARI CREATURES ARE AUTHORED HERE BUT THE CRITTER DRAWING IS NOT
 PORTED -- critter.zig needs emoji_frames.zig, 182 KB of generated bakes that
 NOTES scopes out. The placement data is still ported because it is the world's,
 not the renderer's, and because it is what the exit-corner logic reads.

 We say:

Section: Palette and Dimensions

  conifer-green : Integer
  conifer-green = 1858082

  conifer-gold : Integer
  conifer-gold = 13605400

  conifer-red : Integer
  conifer-red = 11680298

  small-height : Real
  small-height = 4.5

  big-scale : Real
  big-scale = 1.3

 Metres between trees, and this is FIXED where the TypeScript fixes the COUNT --
 so density, and the speed illusion it creates, is uniform across segments.

  tree-spacing : Real
  tree-spacing = 30.0

  tree-road-offset : Real
  tree-road-offset = 1.5

  tree-start-inset : Real
  tree-start-inset = 6.0

  tree-end-inset : Real
  tree-end-inset = 85.0

  lane-width : Real
  lane-width = 4.0

 Longer segments stand their own tower halfway down.

  mid-tower-min-length : Real
  mid-tower-min-length = 1000.0

  max-trees : Integer
  max-trees = 96

Section: Schemes and Creatures

  Scheme =
   | AllGreen
   | YellowGreen
   | RedGreen

 The big animal parked at a segment's EXIT corner. Elephant is the unmarked
 default; the rest are the rare departures. DuckPond is a hand-drawn scene rather
 than an emoji pair, and NoCreature is the terminus, which has no exit corner.

 The constructor is DuckPond rather than Pond because chapters share one flat
 namespace inside a bundle (CDX3001) and `Safari chapter Pond` is already in this
 port -- a collision that would only appear once something cited both.

  Creature =
   | NoCreature
   | Elephant
   | Giraffe
   | Zebra
   | Rhino
   | DuckPond

Section: Trees and Critters

  Tree = record {
    along : Real,
    across : Real,
    color : Integer,
    height : Real
  }

 A roadside animal: an emoji billboard at along and across-from-centre.

  Critter = record {
    along : Real,
    across : Real,
    codepoint : Integer,
    height : Real,
    face-right : Boolean
  }

  accent-color : Scheme -> Integer
  accent-color (s) =
   when s
    is YellowGreen -> conifer-gold
    is RedGreen -> conifer-red
    is AllGreen -> conifer-green

 Conifers every tree-spacing metres from the start inset to the end-clear zone.
 Even index is green and 1.3 times tall, odd is the scheme's accent. Red trees are
 twice everything; gold three times and set about four times further off the road.

 The pair is emitted together, so the cap is checked two at a time exactly as the
 zig's `n_trees + 2 <= MAX_TREES` does.

  tree-height-for : Integer, Boolean -> Real
  tree-height-for (color) (even) =
    let base = if even then small-height * big-scale else small-height
    in if color == conifer-red then base * 2.0 else (if color == conifer-gold then base * 3.0 else base)

  tree-x-for : Integer -> Real
  tree-x-for (color) =
    if color == conifer-gold then lane-width / 2.0 + 4.0 * tree-road-offset
    else lane-width / 2.0 + tree-road-offset

  fill-trees : Scheme, Real, Real, Integer, Integer -> List Tree
  fill-trees (scheme) (length) (along) (k) (n) =
    if along > length - tree-end-inset then []
    else if n + 2 > max-trees then []
    else fill-trees-pair scheme length along k n

  fill-trees-pair : Scheme, Real, Real, Integer, Integer -> List Tree
  fill-trees-pair (scheme) (length) (along) (k) (n) =
    let even = k - ((k / 2) * 2) == 0
    in let color = if even then conifer-green else accent-color scheme
    in let height = tree-height-for color even
    in let x = tree-x-for color
    in [Tree { along = along, across = 0.0 - x, color = color, height = height }, Tree { along = along, across = x, color = color, height = height }] & fill-trees scheme length (along + tree-spacing) (k + 1) (n + 2)

Section: The Cow Herd

 The boring CONSTANT on every segment, near the start and on the left: a bull
 leading ten cows and four calves in a deterministic jittered grid. No randomness
 anywhere -- the jitter is a sine of the index.

  bull-cp : Integer
  bull-cp = 128002

  cow-cp : Integer
  cow-cp = 128004

  cow-height : Real
  cow-height = 1.4

  calf-height : Real
  calf-height = cow-height / 2.0

  bull-height : Real
  bull-height = cow-height * 1.15

  herd-road-offset : Real
  herd-road-offset = 10.0

  bull-dist : Real
  bull-dist = 24.0

  bull-tree-gap : Real
  bull-tree-gap = 0.5

  herd-gap-behind-bull : Real
  herd-gap-behind-bull = 6.0

  herd-col-spacing : Real
  herd-col-spacing = 6.0

  herd-row-stagger : Real
  herd-row-stagger = 2.0

  herd-row-depth : Real
  herd-row-depth = 5.0

  herd-jitter-along : Real
  herd-jitter-along = 1.5

  herd-jitter-across : Real
  herd-jitter-across = 1.2

 The bull is authored per segment in the route table, like the cat and the pigs,
 so the whole schedule of surprises reads in one place.

  bull-of : Boolean -> List Critter
  bull-of (bull) =
    if bull then [Critter { along = bull-dist, across = 0.0 - (lane-width / 2.0 + tree-road-offset + bull-height / 2.0 + bull-tree-gap), codepoint = bull-cp, height = bull-height, face-right = False }]
    else []

  cows-from : Integer -> List Critter
  cows-from (i) =
    if i >= 14 then []
    else [cow-at i] & cows-from (i + 1)

  cow-at : Integer -> Critter
  cow-at (i) =
    let fi = real-from-int i
    in let col = real-from-int (i / 3)
    in let row = real-from-int (i - ((i / 3) * 3))
    in let along = bull-dist + herd-gap-behind-bull + col * herd-col-spacing + (row - 1.0) * herd-row-stagger + herd-jitter-along * r-sin (fi * 2.7)
    in let across = 0.0 - (lane-width / 2.0 + herd-road-offset + row * herd-row-depth + herd-jitter-across * r-cos (fi * 1.9))
    in Critter { along = along, across = across, codepoint = cow-cp, height = if i - ((i / 4) * 4) == 1 then calf-height else cow-height, face-right = True }

  fill-cows : Boolean -> List Critter
  fill-cows (bull) = (bull-of bull) & cows-from 0

Section: The Pigs

 The DISTRACTION herd is a deep seven-by-seven staggered block near the segment's
 end, on the right, and the rider gawks at one designated pig as he passes. Only
 the first two pig appearances distract -- the novelty wears off.

  pig-cp : Integer
  pig-cp = 128022

  pig-height : Real
  pig-height = 1.1

  pig-novelty-count : Integer
  pig-novelty-count = 2

  pig-dist-before-end : Real
  pig-dist-before-end = 60.0

  gaze-pig-along-offset : Real
  gaze-pig-along-offset = 2.0

  big-herd-cols : Integer
  big-herd-cols = 7

  big-herd-rows : Integer
  big-herd-rows = 7

  pig-col-spacing : Real
  pig-col-spacing = 4.0

 Across-spacing AND the per-row along-shear, which is what slants the herd's sides
 at forty-five degrees.

  pig-row-depth : Real
  pig-row-depth = 6.0

  pig-jitter-along : Real
  pig-jitter-along = 1.2

  pig-jitter-across : Real
  pig-jitter-across = 1.0

  pig-herd-first-col : Real
  pig-herd-first-col = 0.0 - 6.0

  pig-back-row-offset : Real
  pig-back-row-offset = 6.0

 The ONE front-row pig the distracted rider fixes on, so the head-turn always aims
 at a pig that is actually drawn.

  gaze-pig : Real, Real -> Critter
  gaze-pig (length) (lane-half) =
    Critter { along = length - pig-dist-before-end + gaze-pig-along-offset, across = lane-half + herd-road-offset, codepoint = pig-cp, height = pig-height, face-right = False }

  herd-pig-at : Real, Integer, Integer -> Critter
  herd-pig-at (base) (r) (c) =
    let i = real-from-int (r * big-herd-cols + c)
    in let fr = real-from-int r
    in let fc = real-from-int c
    in let along = base + pig-herd-first-col + fc * pig-col-spacing + fr * pig-row-depth + pig-jitter-along * r-sin (i * 2.3)
    in let across = lane-width / 2.0 + herd-road-offset + fr * pig-row-depth + pig-jitter-across * r-cos (i * 1.7)
    in Critter { along = along, across = across, codepoint = pig-cp, height = pig-height, face-right = False }

  herd-cols-from : Real, Integer, Integer -> List Critter
  herd-cols-from (base) (r) (c) =
    if c >= big-herd-cols then []
    else [herd-pig-at base r c] & herd-cols-from base r (c + 1)

  herd-rows-from : Real, Integer -> List Critter
  herd-rows-from (base) (r) =
    if r >= big-herd-rows then []
    else (herd-cols-from base r 0) & herd-rows-from base (r + 1)

  fill-pig-herd : Real -> List Critter
  fill-pig-herd (length) = herd-rows-from (length - pig-dist-before-end) 0

 The small non-distraction row: a front rank of four at the road edge and a back
 rank of six further off. The rider does not gawk at these.

  pig-row-front : List Real
  pig-row-front = [0.0 - 6.0, 0.0 - 2.0, 2.0, 6.0]

  pig-row-back : List Real
  pig-row-back = [0.0 - 10.0, 0.0 - 6.0, 0.0 - 2.0, 2.0, 6.0, 10.0]

  row-pigs-at : Real, Real, List Real, Integer -> List Critter
  row-pigs-at (base) (across) (ds) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ds then []
    else [Critter { along = base + list-at ds i, across = across, codepoint = pig-cp, height = pig-height, face-right = False }] & row-pigs-at base across ds (i + 1)

  fill-pig-row : Real -> List Critter
  fill-pig-row (length) =
    let base = length - pig-dist-before-end
    in let edge = lane-width / 2.0 + herd-road-offset
    in (row-pigs-at base edge pig-row-front 0) & row-pigs-at base (edge + pig-back-row-offset) pig-row-back 0

Section: The Route

  Cfg = record {
    length : Real,
    scheme : Scheme,
    turn-deg : Real,
    cat : Boolean,
    pigs : Boolean,
    bull : Boolean,
    terminates : Boolean,
    creature : Creature
  }

 turn-deg is the SIGNED exit turn onto the next segment, positive right and
 negative left. The LAST entry has no turn: it is the terminus, the photo-finish
 line.

  cfg : Real, Scheme, Real, Boolean, Boolean, Boolean, Boolean, Creature -> Cfg
  cfg (len) (sch) (turn) (c) (p) (b) (t) (cr) =
    Cfg { length = len, scheme = sch, turn-deg = turn, cat = c, pigs = p, bull = b, terminates = t, creature = cr }

  route : List Cfg
  route = [cfg 500.0 AllGreen 50.0 False False False False Elephant, cfg 320.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 70.0) True False False False Elephant, cfg 400.0 AllGreen 20.0 False True False False Elephant, cfg 300.0 YellowGreen 20.0 False False False False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 70.0) False False True False Giraffe, cfg 300.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 70.0) False False True False Elephant, cfg 1200.0 RedGreen 80.0 False False True False Rhino, cfg 300.0 AllGreen 15.0 False False True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 70.0) False False True False Elephant, cfg 800.0 AllGreen 15.0 False False True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen 15.0 False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen 15.0 False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 RedGreen 15.0 True True True False DuckPond, cfg 400.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 50.0) False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen 50.0 False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 RedGreen (0.0 - 50.0) False False True False Zebra, cfg 300.0 AllGreen 50.0 False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 AllGreen (0.0 - 50.0) False True True False Elephant, cfg 300.0 RedGreen 0.0 True True True True NoCreature]

Section: Building

 The smallest right-side tree `along` at or after `desired` -- the cat tucks just
 past it, so a tree stands between the rider and the cat. The zig starts at
 infinity and takes the minimum; this threads a Boolean instead, because Codex has
 no infinity literal and a sentinel is a worse answer than saying what is meant.

  next-tree-loop : List Tree, Real, Integer, Boolean, Real -> Real
  next-tree-loop (ts) (desired) (i) (found) (best) =
    if i >= list-length ts then (if found then best else desired)
    else next-tree-step ts desired i found best

  next-tree-step : List Tree, Real, Integer, Boolean, Real -> Real
  next-tree-step (ts) (desired) (i) (found) (best) =
    let t = list-at ts i
    in let take = if t.across > 0.0 then (if t.along >= desired then (if found then t.along < best else True) else False) else False
    in if take then next-tree-loop ts desired (i + 1) True t.along
       else next-tree-loop ts desired (i + 1) found best

  next-tree-along : List Tree, Real -> Real
  next-tree-along (ts) (desired) = next-tree-loop ts desired 0 False 0.0

 Each segment's heading vs north, accumulated from the previous segments' exit
 turns. Segment 0 is zero.

  heading-step : Integer -> Real
  heading-step (i) =
    let c = list-at route i
    in (if c.turn-deg >= 0.0 then 1.0 else 0.0 - 1.0) * (real-abs c.turn-deg * deg)

  heading-at : Integer -> Real
  heading-at (i) = if i <= 0 then 0.0 else heading-at (i - 1) + heading-step (i - 1)

 How many pig legs have appeared at or before this one -- the first
 pig-novelty-count of them are the DISTRACTION herds.

  pig-count-to : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pig-count-to (i) (acc) =
    if i <= 0 then acc
    else pig-count-to (i - 1) (if (list-at route (i - 1)).pigs then acc + 1 else acc)

  Segment = record {
    length : Real,
    width : Real,
    trees : List Tree,
    cows : List Critter,
    pigs : List Critter,
    pigs-distract : Boolean,
    exit-angle : Real,
    exit-right : Boolean,
    exit-to : Integer,
    commit-along : Real,
    north-heading : Real,
    has-mid-tower : Boolean,
    has-cat : Boolean,
    cat : Cat,
    terminates : Boolean,
    exit-creature : Creature
  }

 The rider crosses the next segment's inner-edge extension half-a-width over
 tan-theta before the segment's end and commits to the turn. The terminus has no
 turn, so he commits at the end.

  segment-at : Integer -> Segment
  segment-at (i) =
    let c = list-at route i
    in let angle = real-abs c.turn-deg * deg
    in let trees = fill-trees c.scheme c.length tree-start-inset 0 0
    in let distract = if c.pigs then pig-count-to (i + 1) 0 <= pig-novelty-count else False
    in Segment {
      length = c.length,
      width = lane-width,
      trees = trees,
      cows = fill-cows c.bull,
      pigs = if c.pigs then (if distract then fill-pig-herd c.length else fill-pig-row c.length) else [],
      pigs-distract = distract,
      exit-angle = angle,
      exit-right = c.turn-deg >= 0.0,
      exit-to = if c.terminates then i else i + 1,
      commit-along = if c.terminates then c.length else c.length - (lane-width / 2.0) / r-tan angle,
      north-heading = heading-at i,
      has-mid-tower = c.length > mid-tower-min-length,
      has-cat = c.cat,
      cat = cat-make (lane-width / 2.0) tree-road-offset (next-tree-along trees cat-along),
      terminates = c.terminates,
      exit-creature = if c.terminates then NoCreature else c.creature
    }

 THE CAT IS BUILT FOR EVERY SEGMENT, not only the ones that have one. The zig
 leaves the field `undefined` when has_cat is false, which Codex has no way to
 say and no reason to want; building it costs one record and reading it when
 has-cat is false is the caller's error either way. Nothing observable changes.

  segments-from : Integer -> List Segment
  segments-from (i) =
    if i >= list-length route then []
    else [segment-at i] & segments-from (i + 1)

  build-world : List Segment
  build-world = segments-from 0

Section: Distances

 The sum of the segment lengths, with intersection arcs ignored as negligible --
 the distance the truck's chase schedule lerps its lead down over.

  course-length-from : List Segment, Integer -> Real
  course-length-from (ss) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ss then 0.0
    else (list-at ss i).length + course-length-from ss (i + 1)

  course-length : List Segment -> Real
  course-length (ss) = course-length-from ss 0

 How far along the course a point sits: the lengths of all earlier segments in
 route order, plus the along. The segments ARE in route order, so the prefix is
 just a sum.

  route-distance-from : List Segment, Integer, Integer -> Real
  route-distance-from (ss) (seg) (i) =
    if i >= seg then 0.0
    else (list-at ss i).length + route-distance-from ss seg (i + 1)

  route-distance : List Segment, Integer, Real -> Real
  route-distance (ss) (seg) (along) = route-distance-from ss seg 0 + along

Chapter: Grade

 The reusable grader: compare a flattened list of values against gold and say
 WHERE the first disagreement is.

 Every seam in this port reduces to the same shape -- a list of Reals, a list of
 Integers, a list of Booleans -- so one grader serves pond, the camera, and later
 the draw-command buffer, which is itself a flat list. Flattening at the seam is
 what makes that true.

 The judge is built out of nothing it judges: g-abs duplicates real-abs on
 purpose. DeviceMath's own accuracy test records why -- when its judge called the
 chapter under test, collapsing that chapter left every cell reading ok.

 A failure names the INDEX, not just BAD. `show` on a Real is refused by the zig
 plug, which needs a __real_to_text it does not have, so a Real cannot be printed
 to say what it was; an Integer index can, and it points at one value instead of
 at a whole seam. When real-to-int lands, this is the chapter that grows a
 scaled-integer dump and the tolerance argument goes away.

 We say:

Section: Judging Reals

  g-abs : Real -> Real
  g-abs (x) = if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - x else x

  first-real-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-real-diff (got) (want) (tol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if g-abs (list-at got i - list-at want i) > tol then i
    else first-real-diff got want tol (i + 1)

  grade-reals : Text, List Real, List Real, Real -> Text
  grade-reals (name) (got) (want) (tol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-real-diff got want tol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Section: Judging Reals Relatively

 A RELATIVE tolerance, scaled by the larger of 1 and the wanted magnitude -- the
 form DeviceMath's own accuracy test uses. Computed values need it: the port
 works in Real (f64) while the game computes in f32, so a projected pixel and a
 focal length disagree in proportion to their size, not by a fixed amount. An
 absolute tolerance that passes a 0.9 duck height would wave through a 4-pixel
 error on a 685-pixel focal.

  g-max : Real, Real -> Real
  g-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  first-rel-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-rel-diff (got) (want) (tol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else let w = list-at want i
      in if g-abs (list-at got i - w) > tol * g-max 1.0 (g-abs w) then i
      else first-rel-diff got want tol (i + 1)

  grade-rel : Text, List Real, List Real, Real -> Text
  grade-rel (name) (got) (want) (tol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-rel-diff got want tol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Section: Judging Screen Coordinates

 A MIXED tolerance: an absolute floor plus a relative term, which is the only form
 that fits a projected coordinate. Neither half works alone, and both failures were
 measured rather than guessed.

 A RELATIVE gate fails near the horizon. A screen y is 300 minus a term of about
 the same size, so a y of 20.6 is a small difference of two large numbers and
 cancellation amplifies f32's relative error by the ratio between them -- fifteen
 times epsilon on the first cone tier of a near tree.

 An ABSOLUTE gate fails far off screen. Projecting a point 37 m to the side at the
 0.4 m near plane puts x at about -62929, where one f32 ulp is already 0.0075 px --
 seven times a thousandth-of-a-pixel floor, before any port is involved.

 The floor governs coordinates near zero and on the canvas; the relative term
 governs the far-off-screen ones the near plane throws out. At a hundred pixels the
 gate is about a thousandth of a pixel, which is the precision worth asserting.

  grade-px : Text, List Real, List Real, Real, Real -> Text
  grade-px (name) (got) (want) (atol) (rtol) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-px-diff got want atol rtol 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

  first-px-diff : List Real, List Real, Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  first-px-diff (got) (want) (atol) (rtol) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else let w = list-at want i
      in if g-abs (list-at got i - w) > atol + rtol * g-abs w then i
      else first-px-diff got want atol rtol (i + 1)

Section: Judging Integers

  first-int-diff : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  first-int-diff (got) (want) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if list-at got i /= list-at want i then i
    else first-int-diff got want (i + 1)

  grade-ints : Text, List Integer, List Integer -> Text
  grade-ints (name) (got) (want) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-int-diff got want 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i & " got " & show (list-at got i) & " want " & show (list-at want i)

Section: Judging Booleans

  bool-eq : Boolean, Boolean -> Boolean
  bool-eq (a) (b) = if a then b else if b then False else True

  first-bool-diff : List Boolean, List Boolean, Integer -> Integer
  first-bool-diff (got) (want) (i) =
    if i >= list-length got then 0 - 1
    else if bool-eq (list-at got i) (list-at want i) then first-bool-diff got want (i + 1)
    else i

  grade-bools : Text, List Boolean, List Boolean -> Text
  grade-bools (name) (got) (want) =
    if list-length got /= list-length want
      then name & " BAD length " & show (list-length got) & " want " & show (list-length want)
      else let i = first-bool-diff got want 0
        in if i < 0 then name & " ok " & show (list-length got)
           else name & " BAD at " & show i

Chapter: WorldGold

 GENERATED by harness/gen_gold.py from probe/probe_world.zig.
 Every value here came from importing the real game module unmodified.
 Do not edit: the next harness run overwrites this file.

 We say:

Section: Gold

  g-w-nsegs : List Integer
  g-w-nsegs = [19]

  g-w-length : List Real
  g-w-length = [500.0, 320.0, 400.0, 300.0, 300.0, 300.0, 1200.0, 300.0, 300.0, 800.0, 300.0, 300.0, 300.0, 400.0, 300.0, 300.0, 300.0, 300.0, 300.0]

  g-w-ntrees : List Integer
